<section class="knowledge-hero philosophy-hero"><div class="knowledge-hero-copy"><nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/index.html">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/understanding.html">Foundations</a><span>/</span><span>Philosophy</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">UNDERSTANDING SANATAN DHARMA / A FIELD GUIDE</p><h1>Four aims.<br><em>Three qualities.<br>Six ways of seeing.</em></h1><p class="lead">The Purusharthas ask what human life may seek. The Gunas describe changing tendencies within nature and mind. The Darshanas ask how knowledge, self, world and liberation are to be understood.</p><div class="knowledge-actions"><a class="button" href="#purusharthas">Begin with the aims</a><a href="#schools">Compare the Darshanas</a></div></div><figure class="philosophy-hero-art"><img src="/assets/images/philosophy-guide-hero.webp" alt="Four pathways entering a courtyard beneath three interwoven currents, with six open stone gateways beyond" width="1536" height="1024"><figcaption>FOUR AIMS · THREE QUALITIES · SIX VIEWPOINTS<span>AI-GENERATED SYMBOLIC INTERPRETATION</span></figcaption></figure></section>
<nav class="knowledge-jumps" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#orientation">How the three maps relate</a><a href="#purusharthas">Four Purusharthas</a><a href="#gunas">Three Gunas</a><a href="#schools">Six Darshanas</a><a href="#reading">A way to read</a></nav>
<section class="section philosophy-orientation" id="orientation"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">THREE MAPS / DIFFERENT QUESTIONS</p><h2>Related,<br><em>not interchangeable.</em></h2></div><p>These teachings can illuminate one another, but they do not form one universal ladder. Each begins with a different kind of question.</p></div><div class="philosophy-map"><article><span>04</span><h3>Purusharthas</h3><p><strong>Direction:</strong> What may a human life responsibly seek?</p><a href="#purusharthas">Dharma · Artha · Kama · Moksha</a></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Gunas</h3><p><strong>Condition:</strong> What tendencies are shaping perception and action now?</p><a href="#gunas">Sattva · Rajas · Tamas</a></article><article><span>06</span><h3>Darshanas</h3><p><strong>Inquiry:</strong> How are knowledge, reality, practice and freedom understood?</p><a href="#schools">Six classical viewpoints</a></article></div></section>
<section class="section philosophy-section" id="purusharthas"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / FOUR PURUSHARTHAS</p><h2>A life with<br><em>more than one aim.</em></h2></div><p>Purushartha means an aim or value of human life. The four are often read together so that worldly flourishing is guided by responsibility and opened toward freedom.</p></div><ol class="aim-grid"><li><span>01 · धर्म</span><h3>Dharma</h3><p>Ethical order, responsibility and fitting conduct. Dharma gives the other aims a moral and relational frame.</p><a href="/glossary.html#dharma">Open the glossary entry</a></li><li><span>02 · अर्थ</span><h3>Artha</h3><p>Material well-being, capability, security and the means needed to sustain life and responsibility.</p></li><li><span>03 · काम</span><h3>Kama</h3><p>Desire, affection, beauty, pleasure and meaningful enjoyment—pursued without abandoning dharma.</p></li><li><span>04 · मोक्ष</span><h3>Moksha</h3><p>Liberation from samsara. Schools differ deeply on the self, ultimate reality and what liberation means.</p><a href="/glossary.html#moksha">Open the glossary entry</a></li></ol><p class="context-line"><strong>Not a rigid staircase:</strong> the four aims can overlap across a life. Texts and traditions differ in how they balance household life, renunciation and liberation.</p></section>
<section class="section philosophy-section" id="gunas"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / THREE GUNAS</p><h2>Qualities in<br><em>dynamic relationship.</em></h2></div><p>Sattva, rajas and tamas belong to prakriti—nature in its material and psychological dimensions. Bhagavata Purana 11.25 describes their tendencies and mixtures.</p></div><div class="guna-grid"><article><span lang="sa">सत्त्व</span><h3>Sattva</h3><p>Clarity, balance, illumination and composure. Sattva can support knowledge, yet the texts still treat it as a guna rather than liberation itself.</p><small>ASK: WHAT MAKES THE SITUATION CLEARER?</small></article><article><span lang="sa">रजस्</span><h3>Rajas</h3><p>Activity, restlessness, striving and attachment to results. It moves, seeks and builds, but can keep desire circling.</p><small>ASK: WHAT IS DRIVING THE ACTIVITY?</small></article><article><span lang="sa">तमस्</span><h3>Tamas</h3><p>Inertia, obscurity, heaviness and confusion. Rest has a place; tamas becomes binding when it clouds discernment or prevents needed action.</p><small>ASK: WHAT IS BEING AVOIDED OR LEFT UNSEEN?</small></article></div><figure class="philosophy-inline-art"><img src="/assets/images/three-gunas-loom.webp" alt="Ivory, terracotta and indigo threads continually exchanging prominence on an unfinished handloom textile" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"><figcaption>THE GUNAS AS CHANGING, INTERWOVEN TENDENCIES · AI-GENERATED SYMBOLIC INTERPRETATION</figcaption></figure><div class="reader-note"><span>NOT PERSONALITY LABELS</span><p>The gunas mingle and change. Calling a whole person “sattvic,” “rajasic” or “tamasic” can flatten a teaching intended to sharpen observation of changing states, choices and habits.</p></div></section>
<section class="section philosophy-section" id="schools"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / SIX CLASSICAL DARSHANAS</p><h2>Six ways of<br><em>seeing and reasoning.</em></h2></div><p>Darshana can mean a viewpoint or philosophical system. The classical six are often presented in three historical pairs, but each school has internal debates and later developments.</p></div><div class="school-pairs">
<article><p class="eyebrow">REASON &amp; CATEGORIES</p><div><section><h3>Nyaya</h3><p>Examines reliable knowledge, inference, debate and error. The Nyaya Sutra opens by linking knowledge of its categories with the highest good.</p><p><strong>Key concern:</strong> How do we know that a claim is warranted?</p></section><section><h3>Vaisheshika</h3><p>Analyses reality through categories such as substance, quality, motion, universality, particularity and inherence.</p><p><strong>Key concern:</strong> What kinds of things exist?</p></section></div></article>
<article><p class="eyebrow">DISCERNMENT &amp; PRACTICE</p><div><section><h3>Samkhya</h3><p>Distinguishes consciousness (purusha) from material nature (prakriti) and analyses the tattvas through which experience unfolds.</p><p><strong>Key concern:</strong> What changes, and what is the witnessing principle?</p></section><section><h3>Yoga</h3><p>Develops disciplined practice for stilling mental movements and establishing clear seeing. Classical Yoga shares important categories with Samkhya while giving Ishvara a distinctive role.</p><p><strong>Key concern:</strong> How is discernment made lived and steady?</p></section></div></article>
<article><p class="eyebrow">INTERPRETING THE VEDA</p><div><section><h3>Purva Mimamsa</h3><p>Investigates Vedic injunction, ritual action, language and dharma, developing exact methods of textual interpretation.</p><p><strong>Key concern:</strong> How does sacred language disclose right action?</p></section><section><h3>Vedanta</h3><p>Builds on the Upanishads, Bhagavad Gita and Brahma Sutras. Advaita, Vishishtadvaita, Dvaita and other Vedanta lineages differ on self, world, Brahman and liberation.</p><p><strong>Key concern:</strong> What is ultimate reality, and how is the self related to it?</p></section></div></article>
</div><div class="reader-note"><span>DO NOT CONFUSE THE MAPS</span><p>The six darshanas are philosophical systems. Shaiva, Vaishnava, Shakta and Smarta are broad religious traditions. A person or lineage may draw from more than one philosophical vocabulary.</p></div></section>
<section class="section reading-method" id="reading"><p class="eyebrow">04 / READ PHILOSOPHY SLOWLY</p><h2>Carry four<br><em>questions into a text.</em></h2><ol><li><span>01</span><p><strong>What counts as knowledge?</strong> Notice which sources of knowing the author accepts.</p></li><li><span>02</span><p><strong>What is the self?</strong> “Atman,” “purusha” and “jiva” do not function identically everywhere.</p></li><li><span>03</span><p><strong>What causes bondage?</strong> Ignorance, misidentification, karma and attachment receive different emphasis.</p></li><li><span>04</span><p><strong>What is liberation?</strong> Do not assume every school describes the same final state.</p></li></ol><div class="knowledge-link-grid"><a href="/kapila-devahuti.html"><span>SAMKHYA IN NARRATIVE</span>Kapila and Devahuti</a><a href="/avadhuta-teachers.html"><span>LEARNING FROM EXPERIENCE</span>The Avadhuta's teachers</a><a href="/scriptures.html#upanishads"><span>THE VEDIC DIALOGUES</span>Explore the Upanishads</a></div></section>
